HEADIF:Large argument numbers;gnu;bsd
TEXT
Numbers can be larger than 0 -- 9 (up to 9999) to select the arguments. 

The second line defines the special characters for the arguments. This is missing the fifth character.
It is thus not possible to select arguments from other stacks than the first stack.

END
CODE
0_chars_args:4$*@#;101;102;103;m4exec
0_chars_args:4$*@#;101;102;103;m4builtin
END
HEADIF:Argument with single number;v7;posix
TEXT
Numbers can only be 0 -- 9 to select the arguments. 

END
CODE
0_chars_args:1$*@#;101;102;103;m4exec
0_chars_args:1$*@#;101;102;103;m4builtin
END
IF:all
CODE
0_chars_args:1$*@#$;111;112;103
END
HEADIF:Quoting in default macro set;all
TEXT

In the next part a macro with a pattern is used to have quoting in the macros in the
default macro set @code{0} operational.
The quoting is thus not a builtin function in @code{m0}. 

END
CODE
0_set_var:111;++-+
0_set_var:112;--+-
0_pattern:0string;[@00-@ff]~;stack_b 1 stack_a begin-1
0_pattern:0string;++-+;stack_b 1 +
0_pattern:0string;--+-;stack_b 1 - dup =0 if stack_a end stop endif
0_define:++-+;$1;;nn00 ;0string
END
TEXT

And defining a beginning pattern to be copied.

END
CODE
0_pattern:0start;[@00-@ff]~;stack_b 1 stack_a begin-1
END
HEADIF:Collecting arguments of macros;all
TEXT

The collecting of arguments in @code{m4} macros is executed with a pattern named @code{m4} 
containing small programs.

END
CODE
0_specialchar:(;[@00-@27@29-@ff]
0_pattern:m4;[\(]~;stack_b argpos =0 ifthen abort 
0_pattern:m4;[@00-@ff]~;stack_c 0
0_pattern:m4;(;stack_c 1 +
0_pattern:m4;[(,][@00- ]*[!-@ff];stack_a begin-1 ;1
0_pattern:m4;);stack_c 1 - dup =0 if stack_a end stop endif
0_pattern:m4;,;stack_c dup <=1 if stack_a end endif
END
HEADIF:define;all
TEXT

First some special characters are defined to make the definition of macros easier.

The "@code{\ }" is used at the end of a macro to mean all characters except characters valid for a name.
This ends the detection of a macro name. It is used for all macros which have optional arguments.
Macros which must be followed by arguments end with a "@code{(}".

The "@code{\B}" is used at the start of a macro to mean all characters except characters valid for a name.
This is used to not start a macro inside a word.

END
CODE
0_specialchar: ;[@00-@2f@3a-@40@5b-@5e@60@7b-@ff]
0_specialchar:B;[@00-@40@5b-@5e@60@7b-@ff]
END
TEXT

The @code{define} of @code{m4} can not be directly linked to the function @code{define}.
In @code{m4} a builtin macro can be named by using @code{defn}. These builtin
macros should be handled differently from a user macro.
Some code is necessary to handle the two cases.

Without the builtin definition, the @code{define} could be directly linked as:
@example
@group
@verbatim

0_define:\Bdefine(;;define;nr11 ;m4;;++-+stack_d "" putarg3
"rr11" putarg4 "m4" putarg5 "" putarg6 "" putarg7 "m4exec"
putarg8 "\B" getarg1 cat "\ " cat putarg1--+-;m4exec

@end verbatim
@end group
@end example
In this example a program, executed in the macro, sets 
default values for the @code{define} function.

In this emulation the @code{defn} returns the information of the builtin macro on stack h.
When a builtin is returned by the @code{defn} stack h holds:
@itemize
@item empty or true (1)
@item name of builtin macro
@item info of builtin macro
@end itemize

A macro to check for this and select the correct option is defined to handle
this. The macro is actually a large program that:
@itemize
@item Checks if there are no arguments or if the macro name is empty. If this
is the case do not define a macro and stop.
@item Copies the arguments to separate stacks for later use, stack e holds argument 1,
stack f holds argument 2.
@item Checks if stack h holds a true (1) for a builtin macro,
@item for builtin macro does 2x a copy, one to copy the builtin and 
one to copy the macro in the m4exec macro set,
@item or defines a new macro if a user macro is defined.
@end itemize

END
CODE
0_program:define;++-+ 
stack_e argnum =0 ifthen stop
getarg1 dup "" strcmp ifthen stop
stack_f getarg2
stack_h if
stack_a pop_to_2 copyfr_h swap "m4builtin" "m4builtin" "copy" fun_call
pop_to_1 "\B" copyfr_h cat stack_h pop stack_a copyfr_h cat  "\B" copyfr_e cat
copyfr_h  cat "m4exec" "m4exec" "copy" fun_call 
else
stack_a pop_to_1 "\B" copyfr_e cat "\ " cat 
copyfr_f "" "rr11" "m4" "" "" "m4exec" "define" fun_call 
endif --+-
END
TEXT

The program uses function calls, the macro is further empty because of this.

The @code{m4builtin} set is used to hold the information of the new builtin macro.
This has as a side effect that this new macro can also be called using the @code{m4}
@code{builtin}, which is not possible in @code{m4}.

END
CODE
0_define:\Bdefine(;;;nr11 ;m4;; @define ;m4exec
0_define:define;;;nn00;m4;; @define ;m4builtin
0_setinfo:define;(;m4builtin
END
HEADIF:pushdef;posix;gnu;bsd
TEXT
Similar to the @code{define}. The only difference is the use of push instead of define.

END
CODE
0_program:push;++-+ 
stack_e argnum =0 ifthen stop
getarg1 dup "" strcmp ifthen stop
stack_f getarg2
stack_h if
stack_a pop_to_2 copyfr_h swap "m4builtin" "m4builtin" "pushcopy" fun_call
pop_to_1 "\B" copyfr_h cat stack_h pop stack_a copyfr_h cat  "\B" copyfr_e cat
copyfr_h  cat "m4exec" "m4exec" "pushcopy" fun_call 
else
stack_a pop_to_1 "\B" copyfr_e cat "\ " cat 
copyfr_f "" "rr11" "m4" "" "" "m4exec" "push" fun_call 
endif --+-
0_define:\Bpushdef(;;;nr11 ;m4;; @push ;m4exec
0_define:pushdef;;;nn00;m4;; @push ;m4builtin
0_setinfo:pushdef;(;m4builtin
END
HEADIF:defn;posix;gnu;bsd
TEXT
@code{defn} should return the definition of a macro or in case of a builtin something
so that in a @code{define} a new macro can be defined for the builtin.

These two possibilities require a different handling and thus a 
specific argument collection is defined that is similar to
the @code{m4} argument collection, but puts the arguments to
stack b and calls a program to handle a builtin or a normal macro.

A builtin is returned on stack h to be used by a @code{define}.

Stack h is not freed and holds:
@itemize
@item empty or true (1) if a builtin was found
@item name of builtin macro
@item information text of builtin macro
@end itemize

The information text of the macros in the m4builtin macro set 
is used to determine if a macro has optional arguments or not.

END
CODE
0_program:m4defnif;++-+ stack_b end dup "m4builtin" m?_num dup if
copyto_h stack_h m?_info copyfr_b  m?_name 1 free_no else
stack_b pop "\B" swap cat "\" cat "m4exec" m?_num 
dup if
stack_a copyfr_b m?_def cat endif
endif --+-
0_pattern:m4defn;[\(]~;stack_b argpos =0 ifthen abort  
0_pattern:m4defn;[@00-@ff]~;stack_c 0 stack_a ""
0_pattern:m4defn;(;stack_c 1 +
0_pattern:m4defn;[(,][@00- ]*[!-@ff];stack_b begin-1 ;1
0_pattern:m4defn;);stack_c 1 - dup =0 if @m4defnif stop endif
0_pattern:m4defn;,;stack_c dup <=1 if @m4defnif endif
0_define:\Bdefn(;$1;;nr11 ;m4defn;;;m4exec
0_define:defn;$1;;nn00;m4defn;;;m4builtin
0_setinfo:defn;(;m4builtin
END
HEADIF:popdef;posix;gnu;bsd
TEXT

First a program is defined that selects between the two
possible macros. It also sets the macro set.

END
CODE
0_program:undef;++-+ stack_d "\B" getarg1 cat 
dup "\ " cat "m4exec" m?_num if
"\ " cat putarg1 else
"(" cat putarg1 endif
"m4exec" putarg2 --+-
END
TEXT

The @code{popdef} is a link to the @code{pop} function
with the correct input set by the program.

END
CODE
0_define:\Bpopdef(;;pop;nr11 ;m4;; @undef ;m4exec
0_define:popdef;;pop;nn00;m4;; @undef ;m4builtin
0_setinfo:popdef;(;m4builtin
END
HEADIF:undefine;all
TEXT

Similar to @code{popdef}, but with an @code{undefine} function.

END
CODE
0_define:\Bundefine(;;undefine;nr11 ;m4;; @undef ;m4exec
0_define:undefine;;undefine;nn00;m4;; @undef ;m4builtin
0_setinfo:undefine;(;m4builtin
END
HEADIF:Quoting in @code{m4};all
TEXT

In the next part a macro with a pattern is used to have quoting operational in the @code{m4} emulation.

END
CODE
0_set_var:101;`
0_set_var:102;'
0_set_var:103;,
0_set_var:100;$1
0_pattern:string;[@00-@ff]~;stack_b 1 stack_a begin-1 
0_pattern:string;`;stack_b 1 +
0_pattern:string;';stack_b 1 - dup =0 if stack_a end stop endif
0_define:0_get_var:101
;$1;;nn00 ;string;;;m4exec
END
HEADIF:changequote;all
TEXT

The @code{changequote} calls the macro @code{0_changequote} in macro set @code{0}.

This enables the use of macros in the default macro set to perform the change of quotes. 

END
CODE
0_def_mcall:\Bchangequote\ ;0_changequote;0;nr11 ;m4;;m4exec
0_def_mcall:changequote;0_changequote;0;nn00;m4;;m4builtin
0_setinfo:changequote;\ ;m4builtin
0_define:0_changequote;++-+0_undefine:0_get_var:101
;m4exec
0_set_var:101;++-+$1--+-
0_set_var:102;++-+$2--+-
0_copy_pattern:0start;string
0_pattern:string;++-+$1--+-;stack_b 1 +
0_pattern:string;++-+$2--+-;stack_b 1 - dup =0 if stack_a end stop endif
0_define:0_get_var:101
;0_get_var:100
;;nn00 ;string;;;m4exec
--+-;;rn00;0;;++-+stack_d argnum <=1 if "`" putarg1 "'" putarg2 endif--+-
END
