CODE
0_def_mcall:\Bchangecom\ ;0_changecom;0;nr11 ;m4;;m4exec
0_def_mcall:changecom;0_changecom;0;nr00;m4;;m4builtin
0_setinfo:changecom;\ ;m4builtin
0_define:0_changecom;++-+__ifelse(0_get_var:104
,,,++-+0_undefine:0_get_var:104
;m4exec
--+-)0_set_var:104;++-+$1--+-
0_copy_pattern:0start;comment
0_pattern:comment;++-+$2--+-;end stop
__ifelse(++-+$1--+-,,,++-+0_define:++-+$1--+-;++-+$--+-0$++-+1--+-++-+$2--+-;;nn00 ;comment;;stack_a argnum =0 ifthen end-1;m4exec
--+-)--+-;;rn00;0;;++-+stack_d argnum <=1 if "
" putarg2 endif--+-
END
HEADIF:builtin;gnu;bsd
TEXT

Defines a macro call to a macro stored in the first argument.
The macro set @code{m4builtin} is used for calling the builtin macros of @code{m4}.

The macros defined in the macro set @code{m4builtin} are only called from
this macro. Therefore these macros should not recurse their output, because
this is done by the @code{builtin} macro. Since they are never called normally,
the settings for argument collection are irrelevant.

For macros that have a different argument collection than the @code{m4} of
@code{builtin} this represents a difficulty.

The builtin macro itself is also defined in the macro set @code{m4builtin}.
END
CODE
0_def_mcall:\Bbuiltin(;;m4builtin;rr11 ;m4;;m4exec
0_def_mcall:builtin;;m4builtin;nn00;m4;;m4builtin
0_setinfo:builtin;(;m4builtin
END
